# HyperFrames video operations

The primary Line World starter is a native HyperFrames HTML composition with
real local D3, GSAP and SVG. Use its project-owned pure clock and film template
when supplied. The copied Explainer engine remains available for calibrated
mechanisms; inspect its CLI help before using a specific model command.

## Assemble

Use one canonical state model, stable entity IDs and exact data units. Import
native specialist geometry and namespace all identifiers. Put each simultaneous
concept into a section of the complete frame. Keep the wider world persistent
and route the camera rather than creating unrelated slide cards.

Expose `window.renderAt(seconds)` and `window.snapshot()`. Register a paused
timeline which updates that one clock. Keep all timing in the project story.
Every seek, including a backward seek, must reproduce the same frame.

## Preview and render

Put transport, speed, seek and drag controls outside `data-composition-id`.
Default to silence and no visible heading or ending slogan. Configure a native
HTML root with width, height, fps, duration and the required composition ID.

```text
node scripts/hf.ts lint
node scripts/hf.ts check --json
node scripts/hf.ts render --quality delivery --workers 1 --output renders/film.mp4
```

These commands refer to the generated project's launcher, not a global install.
Install its pinned dependency into the project when native rendering is needed.
If it is unavailable, use a real browser to render exact timestamps and encode
the frames with FFmpeg; record that fallback accurately.

## Verify

Inspect reading holds, focus travel, transitions and overview at delivery size.
Check pairwise labels, notation, palette, source values, same-unit domains and
honest proportions. Decode the movie and verify its actual dimensions, fps,
duration, frame count, pixel format and final state. Explain any retained
renderer warning rather than silently marking an incomplete check as passed.

Deliver the MP4 plus editable sources, timing/coverage manifests and the actual
check results. The parent story owns explicitly requested contextual words.
